generates insecure code, introduces vulnerabilities, or fails to follow security best practices can have severe consequences. This has led to the emergence of red-teaming as an essential practice for evaluating code agents before deployment.\n\nRed-teaming, in the context of AI systems, involves systematically probing models with adversarial inputs and edge cases to identify failure modes, safety gaps, and security vulnerabilities. For code agents, this involves testing with malicious prompts, ambiguous specifications, edge-case requirements, and attempts to elicit insecure code generation. The field is still nascent, with many challenges remaining in developing standardized red-teaming methodologies for code agents.\n\nThis paper surveys the current state of code agent technology and red-teaming practices. We organize our discussion around three main themes: (1) the evolution and capabilities of code agents, (2) red-teaming methodologies for evaluating code generation systems, and (3) frameworks for assessing trustworthiness in AI-generated code.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe research landscape surrounding code agents and their evaluation has grown rapidly, drawing from diverse areas including machine learning, software engineering, and cybersecurity. Several foundational works have established benchmarks and frameworks that inform current approaches to code agent evaluation and red-teaming.\n\n### 2.1 Trustworthiness Assessment Frameworks\n\nThe challenge of evaluating AI system trustworthiness has been extensively studied. Wang et al. [1] introduced DecodingTrust, a comprehensive framework for assessing the trustworthiness of GPT models across multiple dimensions including bias, toxicity, safety, robustness, and factuality. This work is particularly relevant to code agent evaluation, as similar multidimensional assessment criteria apply to AI-generated code systems. The authors proposed a systematic methodology for testing GPT models on various trustworthiness benchmarks, establishing evaluation protocols that have influenced subsequent work on code agent security assessment. Their framework demonstrates the importance of comprehensive, multi-faceted evaluation approaches, which is directly applicable to red-teaming code agents where single-metric evaluations often fail to capture the full spectrum of potential vulnerabilities.\n\n### 2.2 Deep Learning Architectures and Code Generation\n\nThe architectural innovations that have enabled modern AI systems, including code agents, build upon foundational deep learning research. He et al. [2] presented Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition, introducing the revolutionary ResNet architecture that enabled training of much deeper neural networks through residual connections. While this work focused on computer vision, the residual learning paradigm has influenced subsequent architecture designs that power transformer-based code generation models. The principles of gradient flow and representational learning from this foundational work contribute to our understanding of how code generation models develop their capabilities and where failure modes may emerge.\n\n### 2.3 Code-Specific Evaluation Benchmarks\n\nRecent work has developed specialized benchmarks for evaluating code generation capabilities. Models such as Codex and Code LLaMA have been evaluated on datasets including HumanEval, MBPP (Mostly Basic Python Problems), and more recently, APPS and DS-1000. These benchmarks assess various aspects of code generation including correctness, efficiency, and adherence to specifications. Red-teaming approaches complement these benchmarks by focusing on adversarial scenarios that standard benchmarks may not capture, such as prompts designed to elicit vulnerable code patterns or attempts to exploit code generation capabilities for malicious purposes.\n\n### 2.4 Security and Adversarial Testing\n\nThe intersection of AI security and code generation has emerged as a critical research area. Recent studies have identified that code agents can be susceptible to prompt injection attacks, where adversarial instructions are embedded within code comments or documentation to manipulate behavior. Other work has examined the propensity of code agents to generate code with known vulnerability patterns, including SQL injection vulnerabilities, cross-site scripting (XSS) flaws, and insecure API usage. These findings underscore the importance of red-teaming as a complementary practice to benchmark evaluation, focusing specifically on security-relevant failure modes.\n\n[1] Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" *arXiv:2306.11698*, 2023.\n\n[2] He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., & Sun, J. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" *arXiv:1512.03385*, 2015.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents and red-teaming is evolving rapidly, driven by the increasing deployment of AI-assisted development tools in production environments. This paper has surveyed key advances in code agent capabilities and the emerging methodologies for their evaluation through red-teaming. We have highlighted the importance of multidimensional trustworthiness assessment frameworks, drawing on established approaches such as those in DecodingTrust [1], and acknowledged the foundational deep learning research that underpins modern code generation systems [2].\n\nSeveral critical challenges remain for future research. First, there is a need for standardized red-teaming protocols specific to code agents that can be consistently applied across different systems and development contexts. Second, the development of adversarial test suites that cover a comprehensive range of security-relevant scenarios is essential for robust evaluation. Third, automated vulnerability detection in AI-generated code requires further research to effectively identify both known vulnerability patterns and novel security issues.\n\nThe interplay between code agent capability and security evaluation represents an ongoing tension. As agents become more capable, the sophistication of red-teaming evaluations must increase correspondingly. This arms race between capability and evaluation will likely define the trajectory of code agent research for years to come.\n\nWe conclude that a proactive, multi-faceted approach to code agent evaluation—combining benchmark testing, red-teaming, and continuous security assessment—is essential for the safe deployment of these powerful tools. By learning from trustworthiness assessment frameworks developed for general GPT models and adapting them to the code generation domain, the research community can establish robust practices for ensuring the reliability and security of code agents.\n\n",
